4. CHANGES — 2026-08-29 SESSION (Edit/Write only; unexecuted AT THE TIME.
   All of it has since been executed and compiles; see 0.9.)
================================================================================

(a) SUBSTANTIVE LOSS FIX — L_pres witness set sized by evidence
    core/inventory.py::present_coverage_loss
    core/objective.py (call site reordered)
    configs/prism.py (new field + new ablation row)
    train_prism.py (threads the new switch)
    METHOD.md §4.4 rewritten, §8 table + a fourth de-confounding note

    THE BUG: k was a constant, max(64, 0.005*65536) = 327. Every present class
    was required to produce 327 confident pixels. A class that truly covers 40px
    can only satisfy that by OVER-CLAIMING ~8x. That pressure falls hardest on
    exactly the rare classes the method exists to rescue (ship, court, tanks,
    mobile home) and surfaces as failure mode 5 charged to the NEIGHBOUR's pixels.
    Root cause: k was doing two jobs. Its only legitimate job is gradient
    stability (k=1 is max-pooling, unstable at batch 1). Area estimation is
    L_area's job and L_area does it from a bound GT provably satisfies.

    THE FIX: k_c = clip(|P_c|, k_min=64, k_max=327), where |P_c| is the same
    propagated pixel count L_area constrains against.
      - large class: cap binds, k_c = 327, behaviour unchanged
      - 40px class: k_c = 64, the stability floor, nothing more
      - class with no propagated region (click fell in a conflict region):
        |P_c| = 0 -> k_c = 64. Correct default: the inventory still asserts the
        class exists, and nothing has been measured about its size.
    Since k_c <= k_max always, this ONLY EVER RELAXES the term. Anti-flooding is
    untouched — flooding is blocked by L_abs and L_area, never by this ceiling.
    |P_c| is read off integer labels so k_c carries no gradient.
    Comparison pinned to fp32 with an int64 arange: in vals.dtype a bf16
    autocast would round 327 -> 328.
    New ablation row `const-k-present` (pres_const_k=True) makes the claim
    testable. READ THAT ROW ON RARE-CLASS IoU AND BOUNDARY PRECISION, NOT mIoU —
    the classes it should hurt contribute least to the mean.

(b) train_prism.py — latent KeyError fixed
    The degradation-warning branch did m['mIoU'] after `best` was set. If a later
    run_eval returns {} it raises. Now reads miou = m.get("mIoU") once and guards
    both branches on `is not None`.

(c) evaluate.py — FOUR defects fixed (this file produces the paper's BEFORE column)
    - removed a call to PrototypeBank.refine, WHICH DOES NOT EXIST. The branch
      could only ever have raised AttributeError. Now reports the flag as
      unimplemented and carries on un-refined. Changes no baseline number: both
      use_prototypes and proto_use_refine_at_eval default to False.
    - replaced an O(86M) Python per-pixel loop with the SAME np.bincount
      expression evaluate_prism.py uses. This is a COMPARABILITY fix, not just a
      speed fix.
    - `gt` was unbound at the _save_prediction_images call if the first batch had
      no mask. Now gt = None.
    - _sample_gt_points: the per-class body sat OUTSIDE `for c in range(...)`, so
      only the last class ever got points, and a maskless image raised NameError
      on an unbound `ys`. Re-indented, plus max(1, h//3) guards and a per-class
      RNG seed.

(d) tools/validate_regions.py — removed two dead variables (sx, sy) my earlier
    edit had orphaned. This tool SETS prop_eps from measurement, so it is
    load-bearing.

(e) configs/base.py::resolve — docstring stated its precedence BACKWARDS. The
    code checks the PACKAGE ROOT first and only falls back to cwd. Corrected.

(f) core/structure.py — documented a real off-by-one I found and cleared:
    region_boundary pads in both directions so a disagreeing pair marks both of
    its members. Exact for the two axis-aligned shifts. For the two DIAGONALS the
    marked pixel is the one diagonally adjacent to both members, i.e. off by one
    step. Harmless because every consumer dilates — but that makes
    edge_radius >= 1 a CORRECTNESS requirement of the diagonal terms, not merely
    a tolerance. Recorded in both docstrings so nobody later assumes exactness.

(g) METHOD.md §8 rewritten earlier this session: added a third column naming what
    configs/prism.py::ablation actually changes, plus de-confounding notes.
      - no-shadow is LOSSES ONLY (stem + head kept; no-invariant-stem is the
        separate representation row)
      - js-homogeneity isolates the sharpening alone, because
        region_js_divergence takes the same `present` projection and the same
        `conflict` exclusion
      - no-region and no-boundary are COMPOUND BY NECESSITY and are labelled as
        such (L_prop/L_hom/L_self are all DEFINED ON the partition; L_bnd is
        one-sided so it is only meaningful beside a smoothness term)

(h) METHOD.md new §9.1 "Reading the before/after table": SPECKLE is identical by
    construction between the two tools (ones-kernel filter2D == boxFilter with
    normalize=False, both tie-break to the lower class index). GHOST and FLOOD
    were NOT identical, so diagnose_failures.py now prints both definitions with
    the eval-matching one marked `<- quote this one`.
    NOTE: the numerals in that section are FORMAT ONLY (<num>/<den> = 0.xxxx).
    I had first drafted it with invented numbers (412/2183, 91/300) and removed
    them — nothing in METHOD.md is a measured value until §10 step 2 has run.


