                       shadow_head_loss squeezes; shadow_edge_mask adds/removes
                       the channel internally. No silent broadcast.
  model/decoder_v2.py  every key the objective reads exists. The non-tensor
                       "classifier" entry passes through f32() untouched.
  core/structure.py    edge_aware_potts_loss, image_edges (per-image quantile;
                       65536 elements is far under torch.quantile's ~16M limit),
                       dilate, candidate_boundary, boundary_precision_loss all
                       correct. forbidden.sum()==0 -> clamp -> loss exactly 0,
                       which is the right reading.
  objective runs in fp32: model(image) is inside amp_ctx(), the objective call is
                       OUTSIDE it. Confirmed by line position.

FALSE ALARMS I RAISED AND THEN DISPROVED — DO NOT RE-INVESTIGATE
  - "finch_init can pick an empty cluster and produce a NaN prototype": NO.
    finch_first_partition relabels union-find roots to CONTIGUOUS ids, so every
    id in 0..n_clusters-1 is non-empty.
  - "classifier.ema_decay assignment in train_prism.py is silently inert": NO.
    ema_decay is a real __init__ attribute (protobank.py:122) and IS read by
    update_ema.
  - "finch_init could shape-mismatch the EMA shadow re-sync": NO. It writes IN
    PLACE under no_grad, preserving Parameter shape and identity.
  - "net.py doesn't emit shadow_prob": WRONG, it does.
  - "load_state_dict(strict=False) silently skips shape mismatches": WRONG, it
    raises. strict=False only tolerates missing/unexpected KEYS.

METRIC COMPARABILITY — RETIRED BY VERIFICATION, NOT ASSUMPTION
  evaluate.py and evaluate_prism.py read the SAME val.json, whose masks live in
  val_masks_remapped (already 0..16), both resize INTER_NEAREST to image_size,
  and both build the confusion matrix with the same np.bincount expression over
  the same 0 <= y < C test. => baseline mIoU and PRISM mIoU ARE the same quantity.

